---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { event, payrollChanges } from "../../../data/jobs202607.mjs";

// Bar chart of monthly payroll changes, computed from the module series
// (which is recomputed from data/sources/PAYEMS.csv).
const W = 720, H = 200, PAD = 10;
const vals = payrollChanges.map((d) => d.change);
const vMax = 220, vMin = -170;
const zeroY = H - PAD - ((0 - vMin) / (vMax - vMin)) * (H - 2 * PAD);
const bw = (W - 2 * PAD) / payrollChanges.length;
const bars = payrollChanges.map((d, i) => {
  const bx = PAD + i * bw + 3;
  const by = H - PAD - ((Math.max(d.change, 0) - vMin) / (vMax - vMin)) * (H - 2 * PAD);
  const bh = Math.abs(d.change) / (vMax - vMin) * (H - 2 * PAD);
  return { x: bx, y: by, w: bw - 6, h: Math.max(bh, 1.5), neg: d.change < 0, label: d.month };
});
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><span>EVENTS</span><span>Jobs report &middot; July 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story">
      <p class="story-kicker">The jobs report &middot; July 2026 &middot; updated August 29, 2026</p>
      <h1>{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek">{event.dek}</p>

      <div class="kpi-strip">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>The economy lost 23,000 jobs in July. Forecasters surveyed by Dow Jones had expected a <em>gain</em> of 83,000.</li>
          <li>The bad news reaches backward: May was revised down from +129,000 to +63,000 and June from +57,000 to +20,000. Three months that once looked solid now read +63,000, +20,000, and minus 23,000.</li>
          <li>The unemployment rate fell to 4.1 percent, but for an unwelcome reason: the share of adults working or looking for work dropped to 61.4 percent, down 0.7 points since January.</li>
        </ol>
      </aside>

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story">
        <p class="toc-label">In this story</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#markets">What the markets said</a></li>
          <li><a href="#benchmark">The August 28 benchmark check</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker">The report</p>
        <h2>What happened</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on August 7 that total nonfarm payroll employment&nbsp;<strong>fell by 23,000</strong> in July and the unemployment rate was&nbsp;<strong>4.1&nbsp;percent</strong>. In the agency's own words, both &ldquo;changed little.&rdquo;
          The declines were concentrated: local government education lost 50,000 jobs
          &ldquo;after showing little net change over the prior 12 months,&rdquo; and retail trade lost
          19,000. Health care, the economy's steadiest job creator, added 22,000, below its
          12-month average of 36,000. <span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
        <details class="receipt"><summary>the record</summary>
          <p>Release: &ldquo;Both nonfarm payroll employment (-23,000) and the unemployment rate (4.1 percent) changed little in July&hellip; Employment in local government education declined by 50,000 in July, after showing little net change over the prior 12 months. Retail trade lost 19,000 jobs in July&hellip; In July, employment in health care continued its upward trend (+22,000) but at a slower pace than the average monthly gain over the prior 12 months (+36,000).&rdquo; <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm" rel="noreferrer">The Employment Situation, July 2026</a> (pinned copy in the records list).</p>
        </details>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          The revisions cut deeper than the headline. May's gain was revised down by 66,000, from
          +129,000 to +63,000, and June's by 37,000, from +57,000 to +20,000:&nbsp;<strong>103,000 jobs</strong> that were reported earlier in the summer are now gone from
          the record. Average hourly earnings rose 2 cents to $37.62; over the year they are up
          3.2&nbsp;percent. <span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          The composition of the loss matters: private employers added 30,000 jobs in July while
          government shed 53,000, so the headline decline is a public-sector story, led by the
          50,000-job drop in local government education. The report landed nine days after the
          Federal Reserve's split decision to hold interest rates, covered in&nbsp;<a href="/events/fed-rate/july-2026/">our July FOMC story</a>.&nbsp;<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker">History</p>
        <h2>Where this sits</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-computed">
          July was the <strong>fifth negative month in the past twelve</strong>, after August,
          October, and December 2025 and February 2026. Over those twelve months the economy added
          a net 316,000 jobs, about <strong>26,000 a month</strong>: a near-stall by the standard of
          any recent expansion, and choppy rather than steadily weak, with swings from minus 156,000
          to plus 214,000 in a single spring. <span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span>
        </p>
        <figure class="rate-chart">
          <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${H + 24}`} role="img" aria-label="Monthly change in payrolls, June 2025 to July 2026: five negative months in the past twelve, ending at minus 23,000">
            <line x1={PAD} y1={zeroY} x2={W - PAD} y2={zeroY} stroke="#8892a0" stroke-width="1" />
            {bars.map((b) => (
              <rect x={b.x} y={b.y} width={b.w} height={b.h} fill={b.neg ? "#c98a1b" : "#7a9cc4"} />
            ))}
            <text x={PAD} y={H + 16} class="chart-axis">Jun 2025</text>
            <text x={PAD + 7 * bw} y={H + 16} class="chart-axis">Jan 2026</text>
            <text x={W - PAD - 56} y={H + 16} class="chart-axis">Jul 2026</text>
            <text x={W - PAD - 4} y={bars[bars.length - 1].y + bars[bars.length - 1].h + 14} text-anchor="end" class="chart-note chart-note-accent">-23,000</text>
          </svg>
          <figcaption>Monthly change in total nonfarm payrolls, thousands, seasonally adjusted. Amber bars are losses. Source: BLS via FRED (PAYEMS), series as captured August 25, 2026.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          The unemployment rate's dip from 4.2 to 4.1 percent is not the good news it appears.
          The release attributes it to people leaving the labor force: participation fell to
          61.4&nbsp;percent and is down 0.7 percentage point since January.&nbsp;<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker">The coverage</p>
        <h2>What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Three outlets' coverage of this report is in our records: CNBC's and CBS's stories from
          release day, and NBC's morning-of preview, captured before the number came out. Checked
          against the release:
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          <strong>Right:</strong> the headline figures. CNBC's minus 23,000, the 4.1 percent rate,
          the May and June revisions, retail's loss of 19,000, health care's below-trend 22,000, and
          the 2-cent earnings gain all match the release exactly. Its explanation of the falling
          unemployment rate, fewer people working or looking, matches the release's participation
          numbers. <span class="chip chip-record">checked</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-computed">
          <strong>Verified against the release's own tables:</strong> CNBC's figures of 40,000
          jobs lost in leisure and hospitality, 53,000 lost in government, a 14,000 drop in
          financial activities, and a 30,000 gain in private payrolls all match the release (its
          text or its summary table). What separates the two documents is emphasis, not
          arithmetic: the release classes leisure and hospitality among industries whose
          employment &ldquo;showed little change over the month,&rdquo; while CNBC headlined the
          sector's drop and attached a cause, &ldquo;a possible consequence of the World Cup
          tournament ending,&rdquo; that appears nowhere in the release. The number is the
          agency's; the explanation is the outlet's speculation.&nbsp;<span class="chip chip-computed">checked</span></p>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          <strong>CBS</strong> matches the release throughout: the 23,000 loss, the 50,000 and
          19,000 sector losses, health care's 22,000, the combined 103,000 revision, and the
          participation explanation for the falling unemployment rate. It adds a second forecast:
          economists polled by FactSet expected a gain of 95,000, even further from the result
          than the Dow Jones survey's 83,000. However it is measured, the miss was more than
          100,000 jobs.&nbsp;<span class="chip chip-record">checked</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          <strong>NBC's</strong> morning-of preview, in our records as captured hours before the
          release, is its own kind of record: it expected hiring to pick up to 83,000 and
          unemployment to hold at 4.2 percent. Both were wrong by mid-morning. The preview also
          fixes the backdrop the report landed in: gasoline at $4.06 a gallon, up 36 percent
          since late February amid the war with Iran, and inflation at 3.5 percent against the
          Federal Reserve's 2 percent target.&nbsp;<span class="chip chip-record">checked</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker">One claim, examined</p>
        <h2>Claim checked against the record</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          <strong>The claim:</strong> &ldquo;The 12-month average [payroll gain is] down to just
          34,000&rdquo; (CNBC, August 7, matching the release’s own “average monthly gain of 34,000 over the prior 12 months”). <strong>The record today:</strong> recomputing from the
          current published series gives about 26,000 a month over the same window. Neither number
          is an error: CNBC's was correct on the data available August 7, and later revisions pulled
          the average lower still. The claim aged in one direction, downward, which is itself
          informative: this year's first estimates have repeatedly overstated the job market.&nbsp;<span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker">The people in the numbers</p>
        <h2>Who feels it</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          The number of people on temporary layoff rose by 153,000 to 921,000. The long-term
          unemployed, out of work 27 weeks or more, now make up 25.5 percent of all unemployed
          people: one in four. The number working part time because they could not find full-time
          work held at 4.8&nbsp;million. For teachers and school staff, the 50,000-job drop in local
          government education landed in the month before the school year begins.&nbsp;<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="markets">
        <p class="sec-kicker">The reaction</p>
        <h2>What the markets said</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          Per CNBC's report in our records: stock futures rose on the news, with Dow futures up
          close to 200 points, and Treasury yields fell sharply. Traders cut the odds of a
          September Federal Reserve rate hike to 44 percent and put October at 58 percent, by the
          CME FedWatch measure of futures prices. A weak jobs number made a rate increase look
          less likely, and markets treated that as good news.&nbsp;<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="benchmark">
        <p class="sec-kicker">Since this page was written</p>
        <h2>The August 28 benchmark check</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          This release said a preliminary benchmark revision would follow on August 28. It did. Once a year the agency checks its survey-based payroll count against near-complete counts from state unemployment-insurance records, and reports how far off the survey was in March. The answer for March 2026: <strong>79,000 jobs too high</strong>, or 0.1&nbsp;percent, with private payrolls 178,000 too high. The average size of this correction over the past ten years is 0.2 percent, so this one is smaller than usual. It does not change the figures on this page yet: the correction is applied to the published series in February 2027, with the January 2027 report.&nbsp;<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
        <details class="receipt"><summary>the record</summary>
          <p>Release: &ldquo;The preliminary estimate of the Current Employment Statistics (CES) national benchmark revision to total nonfarm employment for March 2026 was -79,000 (-0.1 percent)&hellip; The preliminary benchmark revision for total private employment was -178,000 (-0.1 percent). The annual benchmark revisions over the last 10 years have an absolute average of 0.2 percent of total nonfarm employment. In accordance with usual practice, the final benchmark revision will be issued in February 2027 with the publication of the January 2027 Employment Situation news release.&rdquo; <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/prebmk.nr0.htm" rel="noreferrer">Preliminary Benchmark (National), March 2026</a>, USDL-26-1425, August 28, 2026.</p>
        </details>
        <p class="railed rail-computed">
          Private payrolls 178,000 too high and the total only 79,000 too high means government payrolls were counted about 99,000 too <em>low</em>. So the direction this page describes, first estimates overstating private-sector hiring, holds in the benchmark too, at a modest size.&nbsp;<span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span>
        </p>
      </section>
      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker">Open questions</p>
        <h2>What we do not know yet</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Why local government education fell by 50,000 in one month. The release states the figure without a cause, and we will not guess at one.</li>
          <li>How the benchmark will land on the months after March 2026. The preliminary figure covers March only; the month-by-month effect arrives with the final revision in February 2027.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
      <section id="records">
        <p class="sec-kicker">Sources</p>
        <h2>The records</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Everything this page rests on, by kind. We keep a dated copy of each one.</p>

        <h3 class="src-group">Primary documents</h3>
        <ul class="src-list">
          <li><a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm" rel="noreferrer">The Employment Situation, July 2026</a> &middot; released August 7 &middot; every <em>record</em> figure above</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/prebmk.nr0.htm" rel="noreferrer">Preliminary Benchmark (National), March 2026</a> &middot; released August 28 &middot; the benchmark check</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="src-group">Official data</h3>
        <ul class="src-list">
          <li><a href="https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/PAYEMS" rel="noreferrer">Total nonfarm payrolls, monthly</a> &middot; the chart, the five-in-twelve count, the 26,000 average</li>
          <li><a href="https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/UNRATE" rel="noreferrer">Unemployment rate, monthly</a> &middot; the 4.2 to 4.1 move</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="src-group">Coverage checked</h3>
        <ul class="src-list">
          <li><a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/07/jobs-report-july-2026.html" rel="noreferrer">CNBC</a>, release day &middot; the headline figures, the World Cup explanation, the 34,000 claim, the market reaction</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/news/july-jobs-report-unexpected-job-losses/" rel="noreferrer">CBS News</a>, release day &middot; the FactSet forecast of 95,000</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.nbcnews.com/business/economy/july-2026-jobs-report-rcna591138" rel="noreferrer">NBC News</a>, captured the morning of the release &middot; the 83,000 expectation and the backdrop</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/07/long-term-unemployment-job-market-workers-labor-force.html" rel="noreferrer">CNBC</a> on long-term unemployment &middot; context for the one-in-four figure</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="sources-line"><a href="/methodology">How we check</a> &middot; where this page says <em>computed</em>, the arithmetic was done from the saved data series; where it says <em>record</em> or <em>checked</em>, the words trace to a saved document.</p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style is:global>
  @media (min-width: 1360px) { .sheet:has(.story) { max-width: 100%; } }
</style>

